OrthoSheet reads a skid's design parameters off an orthographic general-arrangement sheet: one RO and EDI skid drawn flat in two views, the front elevation over the plan, with a numbered tag on a leader from each part to the parameter it governs and the parameter rows beside the sheet under the same numbers.

- On the site: a `spec` block with `"look": "ortho-sheet"` (renderer `assets/src/blocks/look-ortho-sheet.js`); live on sys-pw "Skid design parameters" (#design). Pass 12 build round E from concept 260 (`design-system/inspiration/gemini/260-p12-sys-pw-design-take2.png`), compared against SkidSheet (concept 224, the isometric GA sheet it replaces on sys-pw; SkidSheet stays in the library). Why: sys-pw drew the same skid isometrically four times (hero, design, documents, options; pass 12 C, G-C5); the flat views break that run, read as an engineer's GA, and the long, low elevation fits a phone's width whole, so every tag shows at once on the phone strip.
- Taken from the concept: the white sheet, the elevation over the plan, square steel-blue tags on leaders keyed to ruled rows, the phone as one elevation then rows. Not taken: its tag numbers (they repeat: 08 three times, 07 twice), its part-name rows (the rows are the page's own parameters), the goalpost frame over the elevation (a closed PP base and two PP stands only), metal pump flanges, the wordmark, the "Show all" fold (every row shows on every width, S7).
- Fields (as SkidSheet): `h`, `caption` (eyebrow and a title block cell), `intro`, `rows [{p, v, m, link, link_label, part}]`, `note`, `cols[2]` (the phone's basis button word). `part` (look-only, DR-018): `membranes` (capacity), `utilities` (the feed stub: battery limits), `exchanger` (feed heat exchanger: membrane design temperature), `pump` (the RO high-pressure pump), `piping` (the product line and the diaphragm sample valve on its tee: materials and dead legs), `joints` (plain lines across the product line at its joints, method-neutral while `[PP joining method]` is open), `panel` (instruments and controls), and in the plan `edi` (sanitization), `frame` (the closed base: budget), `drain` (the reject line: recovery). A row without a part lists with no tag. `fold` is not read.
- Drawing (inline SVG on IsoKit's classes, so IsoKit's colours, grounds and draw-on motion apply): both views share x, so the plan sits straight under the elevation; dash-dot centre lines through the pump and the EDI stack run through both views. Elevation: closed PP base on pads, plate heat exchanger, vertical high-pressure pump, three horizontal RO housings on two PP stands, EDI stack, control panel; the feed stub in at the left, the riser to the housings, permeate to EDI, the product line up and over the panel to a stub at the right (socket-fusion collar, sample valve tee), the reject down past the base to drain. Plan: the same parts from above. PP only: no flanged metal, no handwheels, no legs or dished heads.
- Markup: `div.aq-ortho-sheet[data-pk-sticky]` → `div.__fig[data-pk-fig]` → `div.__sheet` (double rule) → `div.aq-iso-figure.__art.__art--e` (svg `.__svg--e` with leaders `g.__ld[data-r]`, `span.__ring`, tags `span.__tag[data-r]`, phone dots `span.__dot[data-r][data-pk-key]`), `div.__art.__art--p` (svg `.__svg--p`, ring, tags) and `div.__block` (title block: page name, caption, count; `aria-hidden`); then `ol.__rows` of `li.__row[data-r][data-pk-row][data-part][tabindex=0]` (`span.__n`, `h3.__p` with a phone-only `span.__pn`, `p.__v`, `button.__wb`, `div.__m`).
- Desktop: the sheet (7 of 12 columns) holds sticky at `space-24` while the rows run on hairlines (mono number 13 px, parameter 500 17/24, value 15/22, basis 14/20 `ink-muted`, link `flow`). Pointing at or focusing a row (or its tag) lights the row (`flow-tint`), its tag, leader and its part in both views (`flow`), and a ring glides to the tag.
- Tablets (601–860 px): the full sheet, both views and the title block, above the rows.
- Phones (≤ 600 px, `data-pk-bp="600"`): PhoneKit sticky figure, 150 px, no zoom: the elevation alone, cropped to the skid (the tag margins fall outside), with a 24 px numbered dot on each part (the plan's three parts are dotted where the elevation shows them), so all ten dots show at once; the row on the reading line lights its dot and the ring. Rows: number and parameter (15/21) with a 44 px Basis button, the value full width (14/21), the basis and link open under the row.
- Use it for 6–10 design parameters of one skid where each value belongs to a part and the page already shows the skid isometrically. For an isometric sheet see SkidSheet; a tank and its pipework, TankGa; running costs on a skid, CostSkid.

## Motion

Each view draws itself through `AQiso.timeline` (furniture, parts, pipes and leaders, then the water runs the skid once), the plan starting 0.5 s after the elevation; then the tags strike in number order (phones: the dots); rows rise as they scroll in. Once, under 5 s (all marks shown about 3.3 s after the trigger at 1280). The hover ring is ported from Subframe Motion "Inside the image" (atlas-hotspots), MIT, (c) 2026 Subframe: it travels 250 ms `cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1)` and the chosen tag darkens in 180 ms. Reduced motion: the finished sheet; the ring jumps without travel.
